## 5. How V and T are stored to HBM at the end

### H panel write-back (lines 523–528)

```c
for (long idx = tid; idx < (long)sliceH * BLK; idx += NT) {
    int rr = idx % sliceH;
    int c  = idx / sliceH;
    int r  = r0 + rr;
    if (c < b) Hb[(long)r * strideHn + c] = sP[(long)c * sliceH + rr];
}
```

### V^T store — the load-bearing convention (lines 529–543)

```c
// Vt[c, r] = 1 if r==c, panel[r,c] if r>c, else 0.
float* Vb = Vtbuf + (long)bid * strideVb;
for (long idx = tid; idx < (long)sliceH * BLK; idx += NT) {
    int rr = idx % sliceH;
    int c  = idx / sliceH;
    int r  = r0 + rr;
    float val;
    if (r == c)            val = 1.0f;             // implicit unit diagonal of V
    else if (r > c && c < b) val = sP[(long)c * sliceH + rr];   // reflector entry
    else                   val = 0.0f;             // above diagonal -> 0
    Vb[(long)c * strideVk + (long)r * strideVn] = val;
}
```

So `Vt` is laid out `(B, BLK, N)` as `Vt[bid][c][r]` — **the k-th reflector across the panel-row axis `r`**. The trailing reads it as `v_base + krange*stride_vk + rr*stride_vn` (a `(BLK, BM)` tile, lines 2249/2278/2306).

**For your megakernel:** you do NOT store V to HBM — `sP` *is* V (column-major in smem). When the trailing reads `V[k][r]` it must apply the same convention on the fly: `1.0f` if `r==k`, `sP[k*pheight + r]` if `r>k`, else `0.0f`. (The diagonal of `sP` column k holds `beta`, not 1, after finalize — so re-derive the unit diagonal at read time, exactly as the V^T store does.)

T is stored to `Tbuf (B,BLK,BLK)` incrementally during the loop (§4); in your kernel it lives in smem `sT`.

